Title:
Ringer — Static and Live Analysis of Windows Kernel Drivers (.sys)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Ringer is a Go-based tool for static and live analysis of Windows kernel drivers to recover their exposed attack surface, primarily IOCTL control codes and shared ring-buffer/shared-memory artifacts. It statically extracts IOCTLs via x86/x64 disassembly patterns (including MSVC switch jump tables), data/xref scans, and vendor device-type table heuristics, then decodes CTL_CODE fields (DeviceType/Function/Method/Access). It also extracts and classifies ASCII/UTF-16 strings (device names, symbolic links, section names) and detects “gates” (unlock flags) and “handshakes” (magic constants) used in dispatch paths. In live mode it can install/start the driver as a service, enumerate live Object Manager namespaces, and verify findings by issuing DeviceIoControl calls and mapping named sections to dump raw contents. It additionally identifies IRP_MJ dispatch handlers registered in DriverEntry and surfaces access-control related constants/strings referenced by the IRP_MJ_CREATE path. Useful for red teams, pentesters, and driver security researchers triaging third-party drivers and quickly mapping reachable interfaces, with explicit warnings about potential bugchecks during live IOCTL probing.

Technical Focus:
- Windows driver IOCTL extraction (CTL_CODE decoding, dispatch pattern recovery)
- x86/x64 disassembly and switch/jump-table recognition
- PE section scanning with code cross-references (.text/.rdata/.data/.pdata)
- Object Manager enumeration (\Device, \GLOBAL??, \BaseNamedObjects) and live verification
- IRP major function dispatch table recovery (DriverObject->MajorFunction)
- Gate/handshake (unlock/magic constant) detection in driver control paths

Use Cases:
- Rapidly enumerate a driver’s IOCTL interface for fuzzing or manual RE
- Identify device objects, symbolic links, and shared memory sections exposed to user mode
- Validate which extracted IOCTLs/devices/sections exist and respond on a live system
- Triage all loaded drivers on a host and generate a JSON inventory/report
- Spot likely “unlock” sequences and magic values required before privileged operations

Keywords:
Windows kernel driver, .sys, IOCTL, CTL_CODE, DeviceIoControl, IRP_MJ_CREATE, IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL, DriverEntry, MajorFunction, Object Manager, \Device, \GLOBAL??, \BaseNamedObjects, PE parsing, x86 disassembly, x64 disassembly, jump table, shared memory section, OpenFileMapping, MapViewOfFile, ring buffer, gate flag, handshake constant, NTSTATUS, Go, golang.org/x/sys/windows